WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Module 1 asks you for three small
functions in one file.

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:08.800
This video never opens that file. Your
answer stays yours.

3
00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:13.100
What it shows you is the tool that decides
whether the three are right.

4
00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.275
You can run that tool as often as you like
before the deadline.

5
00:00:17.625 --> 00:00:21.725
The three sit under one banner, extension
point 3 of 5.

6
00:00:22.075 --> 00:00:26.800
should retry asks one question. Is this
request worth sending again?

7
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.675
backoff seconds says how long to wait
before you try.

8
00:00:31.025 --> 00:00:35.800
parse json content reads a reply, or says
clearly why it could not.

9
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.225
None of the three needs a network or a
key. They are pure logic.

10
00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:44.275
So the whole assignment runs offline, on
your own machine.

11
00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:49.000
Run the tests before you write anything,
so you know what failing looks like.

12
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:54.275
The output goes through tail, so you see
the summary and no assertion detail.

13
00:00:54.625 --> 00:00:58.475
Eleven failed, six passed. Say that count
out loud.

14
00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:01.675
That is a starting line, not a problem.

15
00:01:02.025 --> 00:01:04.925
Then open the test file itself and read
it.

16
00:01:05.275 --> 00:01:09.800
Every failing name points at a rule the
docstring already gave you.

17
00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:14.250
It is the clearest statement of what your
three functions have to do.

18
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.450
Reading it is part of the job, not a way
around the job.

19
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.725
Here is the same command against finished
work, with the whole output this time.

20
00:01:24.075 --> 00:01:27.150
Seventeen passed, in well under a second.

21
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.675
No request went anywhere. That is what
pure logic looks like.

22
00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:35.750
There is no key to burn here and no rate
limit to wait on.

23
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.175
So run it after every small change you
make.

24
00:01:39.525 --> 00:01:43.250
Now save your proof. Send the same output
into a file.

25
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.825
Nothing printed, because the output went
into the file instead.

26
00:01:48.175 --> 00:01:50.627
So open the file and read it back.

27
00:01:50.627 --> 00:01:53.800
Seventeen passed, and the file is not
empty.

28
00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:57.975
An empty proof file is a submission with
no proof in it.

29
00:01:58.325 --> 00:02:01.150
Check that before you upload, not after.

30
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.675
One warning before you start.

31
00:02:04.025 --> 00:02:06.722
Say you write should retry so it says yes

32
00:02:06.722 --> 00:02:09.550
to every client error, and nothing
crashes.

33
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:15.400
Then a 401 arrives. Your key is not
accepted, and your loop keeps trying.

34
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.672
Your provider's daily limit is gone in
about

35
00:02:18.672 --> 00:02:22.125
four seconds, on a request that could
never succeed.

36
00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:26.350
Then you are locked out until tomorrow,
and the work is due tonight.

37
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:31.150
That is why the rule is narrow. Retry only
what waiting can fix.

38
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.775
Upload two files. Your edited model
client, and your saved pytest output.

39
00:02:37.125 --> 00:02:41.250
Not the whole repository, and never the
file that holds your key.

40
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.275
If you get stuck, paste the test name and
the message on the Q and A board.

41
00:02:46.625 --> 00:02:49.100
Paste the failure, not your answer.

42
00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:54.050
Being stuck costs nobody points. Being
quiet about it costs time.
